\f1\fs22 CEO Dunhilton riffled through the dossier like he was checking a box of chocolates for spider infestation\'97or competent employees. \'93Jamison Hardy,\'94 he murmured, almost reverentially, reciting the biggest name on the cover.\
\'93Indeed, CEO,\'94 Gerry confirmed, keeping it toneless.\
\'93He remains a beacon, a shining example of Airborne ideals. The best of us.\'94\
\'93Indeed,\'94 Gerry agreed, letting the revisionist history pass.\
Abruptly, Dunhilton stood, as if cued by a director off-camera. \'93I need a drink.\'94 He stalked to a cabinet, selected two expensive tumblers, and a mostly full bottle of amber liquid.\
\'93Scotch?\'94 he offered.\
Gerry winced internally. \'93It\'92s midday, CEO, so yes, thank you.\'94 He knew what was coming.\
Dunhilton poured, splashed two fingers into each glass, and returned. \'93Tasmanian Scotch, nothing better.\'94 He raised his glass for a toast, clinked it against Gerry\'92s untouched one, and took a long swallow.\
As a trade delegation leader, Dunhilton had once sued and technically won the right to call Ulyssian spirits \'93Scotch\'94. Later, when it was revealed the naming asset was not contractually owned by Ulyssia, but by a Ulyssian citizen, one Piotr Dunhilton, his star rose yet higher. He\'92d been trading on it ever since.\
This day, bringing out the bottle was an obvious crutch: CEO-as-conquistador, leaning on the legend. The fact that it tasted like concentrated wallaby piss only added to its exclusivity.\
Dunhilton set his glass down with unnecessary volume. \'93Summarise it for me.\'94\
Gerry opened his own copy of the folder, watching Dunhilton gaze at the cover: 
\f2\i HIBReLabs
\f1\i0 .\
\'93Hardy Industries Biological Research Labs,\'94 Gerry baited. \'93You want to know if they actually existed?\'94\
Dunhilton nodded, suddenly wary.\
\'93We have credible evidence they did,\'94 Gerry said. \'93Enough to locate several old sites.\'94 He flipped to a map. \'93HIBReLabs built at least seven major installations in Ulyssia and the Pacific territories, back during our biological arms race with the Sino-sympathetic states.\'94\
Dunhilton started to speak; Gerry stilled him with a finger. \'93But just as they made real breakthroughs, Jamison Hardy shut everything down\'97buried the labs under more concrete than several Ulyssia Harbours could hold.\'94\
Dunhilton\'92s eyebrows climbed. \'93He didn\'92t let the staff out?\'94\
\'93Apparently not. Here\'97\'94 Gerry tapped two file photos. \'93Dr Hamish Hermann and Dr Janine Anstey, entombed alive. There were more.\'94\
\'93What the tax were they working on?\'94\
\'93That\'92s the first kind of bad news,\'94 Gerry said. \'93Let\'92s start with the \'91good\'92.\'94 He waited while Dunhilton topped off both glasses, then continued:\
\'93In one lab, HIBReLabs managed to re-sequence a pararetrovirus: variable specificity. Imagine taking the DNA for an individual or, with a little work, a whole phenotype\'97hair colour, ethnicity\'97and\'85\'94 Gerry let it hang.\
Dunhilton\'92s gaze was blank.\
\'93You could infect a city, a country, the world\'97and only that DNA, or group, dies. That\'92s what we\'92re now calling the TMinusVirus.\'94 Gerry let the word hang long enough to matter.\
Dunhilton\'92s face went flat. \'93To put it plainly: we could wipe out anyone. Absolutely anyone?\'94\
\'93Or any group. Quietly. Tragedy, flu season, no blame.\'94\
Dunhilton took this in, then his brain worked overtime. \'93So that\'92s how we deal with the Elvi.\'94\
Gerry nodded. \'93They\'92re clones; one base genotype. Distribute by air, water, food, or a strategic \'91bacon fat\'92 spill\'97done.\'94\
Dunhilton almost smiled, then caught his reflection. \'93So we just raid an old lab for the stuff and it\'92s solved?\'94\
\'93In theory,\'94 Gerry said. \'93But you need the right team, and we need to be sure we don\'92t... unleash something else.\'94 Gerry flipped to the next dossier, sliding it over.\
\'93TPlusVirus?\'94 Dunhilton read, squinting at the label like it might bite.\
\'93Yes. Don\'92t skim\'97this is the not-so-good good news.\'94\
Gerry made the summary brisk, his voice tightening just a little. \'93Late in their program, Dr Hermann took the same viral platform, and instead of loading it with a killer payload, he loaded it with everything he thought made a human exceptional: tardigrade DNA, volcanic vent fungi, immortal jellyfish, anaerobic bacteria. The result\'97the TPlusVirus\'97optimises every cell in the host. Perfect memory, sight, healing, immunity. Bones denser, nerves sharper, lifespan up, everything.\'94\
Dunhilton snorted. \'93Does it cure cancer?\'94\
Gerry shrugged. \'93Possibly. It\'92s a significant life extender, and makes you functionally\'97for want of a better word\'97invincible. Unless, I guess, you fall from a window.\'94\
\'93Jesus Taxing Christ,\'94 Dunhilton said, glass halfway up. \'93How does it spread? Please don\'92t say networking meetings.\'94\
\'93Sex. Fluid contact.\'94\
Dunhilton exhaled, briefly relieved. \'93Well, that\'92s the missus out of danger,\'94 he muttered. \'93But you mustn\'92t let the field teams release it by accident. This city can barely manage a cold going round the boardroom.\'94\
\'93May already be too late, CEO.\'94 Gerry chose his moment, pushing a batch of photos across.\
\'93Recognise Anthony Chantara?\'94\
Dunhilton grumbled. \'93Interrogation from 
\f2\i Curb
\f1\i0  
\f2\i Your Individualism
\f1\i0 , sure. But\'97\'94\
Gerry lined up a week\'92s worth of photos, one for each morning after Chanta\'92s session with the interrogation team. \'93Spot the difference.\'94\
Dunhilton frowned. \'93He looks younger. Is this some editing trick?\'94\
\'93All these were taken day after day, while Chantara got battered every day. Look at the timeline.\'94\
Dunhilton\'92s hand shook. \'93He\'92s infected.\'94\
Gerry nodded. \'93And he\'92s just been rescued by a battalion of Elvi\'97by order of the so-called Holy Momma.\'94\
Dunhilton\'92s brain began to grind. \'93Who is this Holy Momma?\'94\
Gerry kept it carefully vague. \'93Elvi tradition, CEO. Their original Holy Momma was one Gladys Love, the original Elvis Presley\'92s mother. Grayling Corp programmed the veneration into their ELVIS units right from the start. Sometimes the clones get ceremonial, claim a Holy Momma is rallying them for this or that, but the title shifts with the mood or the year. If there\'92s a current claimant, we don\'92t know who it is. Could just be a new round of Elvi myth-making.\'94\
Dunhilton frowned, not sure who was being handled, him or the truth.\
Gerry sensed the moment shift. Time to seal it.\
\'93I need total resources. The only way this is controlled is if I have direct command. PAPER oversight for intelligence and logistics. TRAMPS for operations.\'94\
Dunhilton hesitated, whirring internally. \'93Done. It\'92s yours.\'94\
Gerry finally sipped his whisky and said nothing. In matters like these, silence was the most diplomatic lie.
